  <!-- B: PLANNING -->
  <section class="block" id="ex-planning">
    <div class="domain-title"><span class="dn">B</span><h3>Planning &amp; songs</h3></div>
    <div class="serves">Serves &middot; <b>Christ-Centered</b> &middot; <b>Excellent</b> &middot; <b>Plan far, hold loose</b></div>

    <p class="lead">We plan two quarters ahead and refine every quarter. At the Q2 meeting we set Q4, at the Q3 meeting we set Q1, and on. Far enough out to build with intention, close enough to keep adjusting as God leads.</p>

    <h3>The weekly build</h3>
    <div class="flow">
      <div class="step"><div class="body"><b>Songs into Planning Center.</b> New songs and their MultiTracks land in Planning Center so everything lives in one place.</div></div>
      <div class="step"><div class="body"><b>Schedule the team.</b> Once blockout dates are in, we schedule vocalists and the band, always leaving an auxiliary slot to grow someone into leading a song.</div></div>
      <div class="step"><div class="body"><b>Lock keys, then the set.</b> Each lead vocalist confirms their key in ChartBuilder. Once keys are in, we lock the set.</div></div>
      <div class="step"><div class="body"><b>Build the set.</b> The locked set goes to the MD for the Ableton build and the arrangement, running roughly two months ahead. Charts and numbers import to ChartBuilder weekly.</div></div>
      <div class="step"><div class="body"><b>Set the vision for the week.</b> Arrangement, flow, tags, choir and acoustic moments go in the notes. We build intentionally, not reactively.</div></div>
    </div>

    <h3>The bigger rhythm</h3>
    <ul class="plain">
      <li>Twice a year we lock 8 to 10 new songs for the next six months, expecting to cut a couple that do not land.</li>
      <li>We keep the working rotation tight, around 14 to 16 songs we already do well.</li>
      <li>We keep a flex list of hymns, tags, and older songs to pull from as the moment calls.</li>
    </ul>

    <div class="strip"><span class="mk">READY AT ALL TIMES</span><p>Some songs are tagged Ready at All Times in Planning Center. Everyone memorizes them and knows the key and the numbers they play them in. Ready at All Times means ready with no rehearsal.</p></div>

    <h3>What makes a banger</h3>
    <p>The live song list lives in Planning Center. What you carry is the filter. A banger clears all four:</p>
    <div class="cards">
      <div class="card"><div class="lbl">01</div><h4>Christ-centered</h4><p>It points to Jesus, with the gospel and sound theology in it.</p></div>
      <div class="card"><div class="lbl">02</div><h4>Tempo conscious</h4><p>Its tempo and energy serve the set. We know what it does to the flow before it goes in.</p></div>
      <div class="card"><div class="lbl">03</div><h4>Moves us forward</h4><p>It moves the church forward in faith and theology. It teaches and stretches, it does not just fill a slot.</p></div>
      <div class="card"><div class="lbl">04</div><h4>Lands the moment</h4><p>It pays off on the bridge, the chorus, or both. The part the room feels and carries home.</p></div>
    </div>

    <table>
      <thead><tr><th>Tier</th><th>What it is</th><th>How it behaves</th></tr></thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr><td class="mono">CORE</td><td>The anchors of the season.</td><td>Played often, carry the most weight.</td></tr>
        <tr><td class="mono">NEW</td><td>Introduced across the six-month window.</td><td>Played heavily early so they land.</td></tr>
        <tr><td class="mono">SECONDARY</td><td>The wider pool that keeps it fresh.</td><td>Played more evenly, by priority.</td></tr>
        <tr><td class="mono">FLEX</td><td>Hymns, tags, older and spontaneous songs.</td><td>Pulled as needed or as felt.</td></tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>
    <div class="strip"><span class="mk">SOURCE OF TRUTH</span><p>Planning Center is the living record for songs, keys, tiers, and Ready at All Times. When this handbook and Planning Center disagree, Planning Center wins.</p></div>
  </section>

  <!-- C: BAND -->
  <section class="block" id="ex-band">
    <div class="domain-title"><span class="dn">C</span><h3>The band &amp; the set</h3></div>
    <div class="serves">Serves &middot; <b>Excellent</b> &middot; <b>Creative</b></div>

    <p class="lead">The Music Director is the musical backbone of the set, a player and a caller at once. From inside the band, the MD steers the dynamics, reads the room, and calls transitions in the <strong>Nashville Number System</strong> so the team can follow the worship leader anywhere. On Sunday the drummer fires the Ableton tracks to the click.</p>

    <div class="cards">
      <div class="card"><div class="lbl">Song</div><h4>Knows every song cold</h4><p>Form, arrangement, and feel, in the keys we play. Eyes up, not down.</p></div>
      <div class="card"><div class="lbl">Flow</div><h4>Calls the moments</h4><p>Builds, drops, holds, tags, and turnarounds, called clearly into a mic.</p></div>
      <div class="card"><div class="lbl">Band</div><h4>Speaks in numbers</h4><p>Changes go out in Nashville numbers, so a call works in any key.</p></div>
      <div class="card"><div class="lbl">Room</div><h4>Reads and responds</h4><p>Feels the dynamics, carries the spontaneous moments, works with audio.</p></div>
    </div>

    <h3>Nashville numbers</h3>
    <div class="chips">
      <span class="chip">1 <span>home</span></span><span class="chip">4 <span>lift</span></span><span class="chip">5 <span>tension</span></span><span class="chip">6m <span>the minor</span></span>
      <span class="chip">hold the 1</span><span class="chip">build on the 4</span><span class="chip">drop to the 1</span><span class="chip">back to the top</span><span class="chip">tag the chorus</span>
    </div>
    <p>Beyond the basics, the MD also calls the more advanced moves that give worship its lift: walk-ups and walk-downs that move the band as one line, gospel turnarounds, snap builds that cut the whole band and rebuild it a layer at a time, and the half-step key bump into a final chorus.</p>
    <div class="flowcall"><div class="call">"Turnaround 7&ndash;5&ndash;6&ndash;4, then 2&ndash;5&ndash;1 into the reprise, up a half step."</div><div class="mean">A gospel turnaround into a key-bumped last chorus. The point is that one short call, in numbers, moves the whole band together.</div></div>

    <div class="strip"><span class="mk">POSTURE</span><p>Servant leadership over showmanship. Stay composed when something breaks, keep the band moving, and hold the set loosely enough to follow where worship goes.</p></div>
  </section>

  <!-- D: RIG -->
  <section class="block" id="ex-rig">
    <div class="domain-title"><span class="dn">D</span><h3>The rig</h3></div>
    <div class="serves">Serves &middot; <b>Excellent</b> &middot; <b>Work with what we have</b></div>

    <p class="lead">A deliberately simple rig. Less friction in tracks, ProPresenter, and setup means more margin for ministry. Ableton runs under the MD and plays like an instrument on the stage, the <span class="mono">Tracks</span> chair on the band.</p>

    <h3>How it is set up</h3>
    <ul class="plain">
      <li><strong>One protected Sunday computer</strong> runs the service and nothing else. Three build machines are where the sets get made.</li>
      <li><strong>The machines stay put; the Ableton license rotates</strong> to whoever is building. People learn on the build machines, so there is no separate training rig.</li>
      <li><strong>On Sunday the drummer fires the tracks,</strong> locked to the click, launching each song from the kit.</li>
    </ul>

    <h3>The weekly build, in short</h3>
    <div class="flow">
      <div class="step"><div class="body">Confirm the final set in Planning Center, then pull new songs from MultiTracks and the Orbit arrangements from the shared drive.</div></div>
      <div class="step"><div class="body">Build each song in its own session, then arrange the full set for seamless transitions.</div></div>
      <div class="step"><div class="body">Collect All and Save, zip, and upload to the team Google Drive. Keep personal copies private, never public.</div></div>
      <div class="step"><div class="body">Export a reference MP3 with that weekend's exact instrumentation.</div></div>
      <div class="step"><div class="body">The MD or Ableton Lead reviews every set before Sunday, then it is loaded and tested on the Sunday computer.</div></div>
    </div>
    <p>On Sunday, the set gets routed out over Dante to the audio console (click, guide, and the instrument tracks), the songs are keymapped so the drummer can fire them, and it tears down like any other instrument at the end.</p>

    <h3>Every Sunday set must have</h3>
    <ul class="checklist">
      <li><span class="box"></span> Official Orbit template</li>
      <li><span class="box"></span> Correct Dante routing, click, and guide</li>
      <li><span class="box"></span> Locators for each song, no missing samples</li>
      <li><span class="box"></span> Tested on the Sunday computer</li>
      <li><span class="box"></span> Reviewed by the MD or Ableton Lead</li>
      <li><span class="box"></span> A build machine standing by as backup</li>
    </ul>
    <div class="strip"><span class="mk">IF THE SUNDAY COMPUTER FAILS</span><p>Fall back in order: a build machine first, then run without tracks. We are never dead in the water. The band can carry a set on its own if it has to.</p></div>
  </section>

  <!-- E: ROOM -->
  <section class="block" id="ex-room">
    <div class="domain-title"><span class="dn">E</span><h3>The room</h3></div>
    <div class="serves">Serves &middot; <b>Excellent</b> &middot; <b>Creative</b> &middot; <b>Accessible</b></div>

    <p class="lead">Audio, visual, lighting, and livestream build the room people walk into. Every one of them is a chance to show people more of Jesus, so the mix, the screen, the light, and the shot all revolve around Him too.</p>
    <div class="pc-note">In Planning Center these are the <b>WP | Production</b> chairs: Audio, ProPresenter, Lights Operator, Cameras, Video Switcher, Livestream Audio, and the LED Wall.</div>
    <div class="strip dark"><span class="mk">DURING A SERVICE</span><p>The Service Coordinator runs the flow, and every area submits to their calls for that service. Leads own their craft; the coordinator owns the clock. Both answer to the Production Director.</p></div>

    <h4 class="sub">Audio</h4>
    <p>The goal is a cohesive, worshipful room, never a muffled voice or a harsh mix. The board is your instrument, so know it well enough to build the moment. Mix from the seats using the board and the iPad, not only from behind the console, and never leave it unattended during a runthrough or service.</p>
    <p>Lean on scenes. Build them ahead of time for the complex moves in a set so a newer tech can carry a service with confidence. Set them during line check one input at a time, drums then tonal then vocals, and keep global safes to only what will not change during service.</p>

    <h4 class="sub">Visual (ProPresenter &amp; LED)</h4>
    <p>Without lyrics on the screen, no one can worship the words, so treat every slide as ministry. Advance on the last word or two of the current line, thinking about how much time someone needs to read it before they sing it. Match the words exactly, keep slides clean, drive from the keyboard, and follow your speakers on the confidence monitor so you can lead them if they lose their place. Setup and care of the LED wall live here too.</p>

    <h4 class="sub">Lighting</h4>
    <p>A distraction-free, engaging room. Light the dynamics, bright and driving for the big songs, warm and pulled back for the intimate ones, and keep it human, never a show about the lights. The rule of thumb is simple: if it is worth it, it is worth it. Safety is not optional: rigging secured, cable taped out of walkways, power load checked, nothing overhead that has not been double-checked before doors.</p>

    <h4 class="sub">Livestream</h4>
    <p>A 3 to 4 camera rig with a switcher, running Resi for the stream. The goal is not broadcast outreach yet. It is a clean, faithful window into the room, so we frame with intention, keep the feed steady, and raise up new operators. The stream is how someone at home meets the same God the room is meeting, so we put our best foot forward.</p>
    <p>Livestream audio comes off the board today as a simple stereo feed. We can build a dedicated broadcast mix when it is worth it, and we can pull the full multitrack over Dante to capture a whole service for later use. We pick the option that fits the moment.</p>
  </section>

  <!-- F: AUDITIONS -->
  <section class="block" id="ex-auditions">
    <div class="domain-title"><span class="dn">F</span><h3>Bringing people on</h3></div>
    <div class="serves">Serves &middot; <b>High Skill</b> &middot; <b>High Character</b> &middot; <b>Accessible</b></div>

    <p class="lead">The path from first submission to serving, or to a gracious no. Two off-ramps are built in on purpose. The Worship Leader and Vocal Lead carry it.</p>
    <div class="flow">
      <div class="step"><div class="body"><b>Audition submitted.</b> The online form comes in, and an automatic confirmation goes out.</div></div>
      <div class="step"><div class="body"><b>First review.</b> The Worship Leader decides whether to move to a live audition, with a verdict inside 48 hours and the candidate contacted within 72. <em>A no is delivered warmly, with growth areas where they help.</em></div></div>
      <div class="step"><div class="body"><b>Prep.</b> Candidates get the tracks, a standard key they can ask to move, in-ears, a number chart, the expectation to come memorized, and a preview of the spiritual interview.</div></div>
      <div class="step"><div class="body"><b>Live audition.</b> Vocalists lead one song and harmonize on another. Musicians play to the track on in-ears. Everyone meets with the Worship Leader for the spiritual interview.</div></div>
      <div class="step"><div class="body"><b>Decision.</b> A verdict within 48 hours, with next steps. <em>The second off-ramp, delivered with the same grace.</em></div></div>
      <div class="step"><div class="body"><b>Assimilation.</b> Paced to the person: shadow for a few weeks, ease in on a song or two, or integrate right away if they are ready.</div></div>
    </div>
    <div class="strip"><span class="mk">TONE</span><p>Every no stays pastoral. People hand us something vulnerable when they audition. How we turn someone down says as much about Orbit as how we welcome someone in.</p></div>
  </section>

  <!-- G: RHYTHM -->
  <section class="block" id="ex-rhythm">
    <div class="domain-title"><span class="dn">G</span><h3>The weekly rhythm</h3></div>
    <div class="serves">Serves &middot; <b>Real</b> &middot; <b>Excellent</b></div>

    <h3>Quarterly rehearsal</h3>
    <p>Its own event on the calendar, a Saturday set aside to learn the next quarter's songs, prep worship-night material, and work the hard transitions. As much about the band as people as about the notes.</p>

    <h3>The Sunday clock</h3>
    <p>Arizona time. Setup and teardown are the job, not a favor. They are the backbone of a portable church.</p>
    <div class="timeline">
      <div class="tl"><div class="t">5:00a</div><div class="e"><b>Setup call.</b> Production and setup crews load in and build the room. Audio line checks as the stage comes up.</div></div>
      <div class="tl"><div class="t">6:45a</div><div class="e"><b>WP Huddle.</b> Relationships first. We connect as people before we execute as a team.</div></div>
      <div class="tl"><div class="t">7:00a</div><div class="e"><b>Worship Runthrough.</b> The band and vocals get the music together, find the arrangements and the pockets.</div></div>
      <div class="tl"><div class="t">7:30a</div><div class="e"><b>General call, then Service Runthrough.</b> The full service, exactly as it runs, with hosts, speakers, and every transition.</div></div>
      <div class="tl"><div class="t">8:15a</div><div class="e"><b>Serve Team Rally.</b> The whole team together before doors.</div></div>
      <div class="tl svc"><div class="t">9:00a</div><div class="e"><b>Service one.</b></div></div>
      <div class="tl svc"><div class="t">10:45a</div><div class="e"><b>Service two.</b></div></div>
      <div class="tl"><div class="t">12:15p</div><div class="e"><b>Teardown.</b> Tear your station down like any other instrument. We are guests in this building every week.</div></div>
    </div>
    <p>The set is planned but held loosely. Follow the worship leader and the MD, and when a moment opens up, we go.</p>

    <div class="conviction">
      <span class="eyebrow">Back to the apex</span>
      <p>If we do our jobs right, no one remembers the production. They remember that <strong>they met God.</strong> Everything in this handbook, top to bottom, exists for that.</p>
    </div>
  </section>

